## Test all (wall-clock time to success)

CPU baselines use the wrapped-objective approach. PSO variants use external
`@elapsed` timing since GPU kernels cannot call `time()` inside the objective.
```julia
tts_results = Dict{String, Vector{Float64}}()

for algo in cpu_labels
    @info "TTS: $algo ..."
    tts_results[algo] = run_cpu_tts(algo)
end

for algo in pso_labels
    tts_results[algo] = extract_tts(pso_results[algo])
end
```

## Success Rate vs. Wall-Clock Time

This is the key plot. The x-axis is a wall-clock time budget; for each budget T,
the y-axis shows what fraction of (function, trial) pairs the optimizer solved
within T seconds. GPU-PSO evaluates thousands of particles in parallel, so it
should dominate on this metric even if it uses more total evaluations.
```julia
all_finite = filter(isfinite, vcat(values(tts_results)...))
t_lo = minimum(all_finite) / 2
t_hi = maximum(all_finite) * 2
time_thresholds = 10 .^ range(log10(t_lo), log10(t_hi), length = 50)

cdfs = Dict(algo => success_rate_cdf(tts_results[algo], time_thresholds) for algo in all_labels)
